  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level low dropping southeastward from the northern plains will \nphase with an open wave out of the southern plains region into a \nrapidly deepening closed low pressure system over the Ohio Valley. \nSurface cyclogenesis will occur over the Florida Keys, moving \nnorthward along the Atlantic Coast. The surface system will rapidly \nocclude, and the deepening surface low moving northward will foster \nan increasing pressure gradient in conjunction with New England high \npressure. For the southern Great Lakes and our CWA, Thanksgiving Day \nis looking like a very windy day with periodic rain. POPs on the \nincrease around 12Z Thursday from the south moving northward. No \nArctic air with this system as the only colder air associated with \nit will be generated from the deepening upper level low, but wet non-\naccumulating snow could mix in with rain along the southern zones \nThursday night. Otherwise, this will be a basin-wide rainfall event \nwhere two day QPF is in the 1-1.5 inch range. Recent dry weather \nwould suggest that flooding would not be an issue, and once into \nThursday night and Friday, the periodic ran becomes more scattered \nin nature with breaks in between. Bulk of the rain likely to fall \nbetween 15Z Thursday and 03Z Friday. Back to the wind, strong \npressure gradient makes for a raw day Thursday with the wind and \nrain, although away from marine considerations, wind headlines are \nnot anticipated right now, but possibilities for such are non-zero. \nThat said, 20-30mph winds expected with some higher gusts to 40mph, \nespecially along the lakeshore in northwest Ohio in ENE winds. Peak \nwinds 18Z Thursday through 06Z Friday, and the Toledo to Sandusky \narea along Lake Erie and inland could be an area where a Wind \nAdvisory sneaks into the fold.",
